  "answer_text": "A **custodial IRA** (often referred to as an \"IRA for Minors\" or a \"Kid IRA\") is an Individual Retirement Account that an adult opens and manages on behalf of a minor child. \n\nBecause minors cannot legally open investment accounts on their own, the adult acts as the **custodian**. However, all the money in the account legally belongs to the child. \n\n---\n\n### How a Custodial IRA Works\n* **Management:** The adult custodian (usually a parent, grandparent, or guardian) manages the account, makes investment decisions, and handles the contributions.\n* **Transition of Ownership:** Once the child reaches the age of majority in their state (usually 18 or 21, though sometimes up to 25 depending on state laws), control of the account officially transfers to them, and it becomes a standard, self-managed IRA.\n\n---\n\n### The Golden Rule: Earned Income\nThe most important rule of a custodial IRA is that **the child must have earned income** to contribute. \n* **What counts as earned income:** Wages from a W-2 job (like working at a grocery store or camp counselor), or self-employment income (like babysitting, mowing lawns, dog walking, or modeling).\n* **What does NOT count:** Allowances, investment returns, dividends, cash gifts, or trust fund payouts. \n\n---\n\n### Contribution Limits \nLike a standard IRA, contributions are limited by IRS guidelines. The maximum you can contribute to a custodial IRA in any given year is **the lesser of** the child\u2019s total earned income *or* the standard IRS annual limit:\n\n* **For the 2025 tax year:** The maximum limit is **$7,000**.\n* **For the 2026 tax year:** The maximum limit is **$7,500**.\n\n*Example:* If a teenager earns $3,000 working a summer job, the maximum amount that can be contributed to their custodial IRA for that year is $3,000. If they earn $10,000, they can contribute up to the maximum IRS limit ($7,500 for 2026). \n\n*(Note: While the contribution limit is tied to the child's income, the money doesn't literally have to come from the child's paycheck. Parents or grandparents can gift the child the contribution money, as long as the total contributed doesn't exceed what the child actually earned that year).*\n\n---\n\n### Types of Custodial IRAs: Traditional vs. Roth\nYou can choose between two types of custodial IRAs, though one is far more popular:\n\n1. **Custodial Roth IRA (Highly Recommended):** \n   Contributions are made with after-tax money. Since minors usually earn very little, they are already in the lowest possible income tax bracket, meaning they won't benefit much from a tax deduction. With a Roth IRA, the money grows **entirely tax-free**, and they can withdraw it tax-free in retirement.\n2. **Custodial Traditional IRA:** \n   Contributions may be tax-deductible, but the funds are taxed as regular income when they are withdrawn during retirement.\n\n---\n\n### Why Open a Custodial IRA?\n* **The Power of Compound Interest:** Time is an investor\u2019s greatest asset. Starting a retirement fund for a child when they are 14 or 15 gives that money 50 years or more to grow. Even a few thousand dollars invested during their teenage years can grow into hundreds of thousands of dollars by the time they retire.\n* **Financial Literacy:** It is a hands-on tool to teach teenagers about the stock market, investing, compound interest, and saving for the future.\n* **Flexibility for Major Life Events:** If you choose a **Custodial Roth IRA**, the principal contributions can be withdrawn tax- and penalty-free at any time (though it is best to let them grow). Additionally, the account owner can eventually withdraw up to $10,000 of the *earnings* tax- and penalty-free to buy their first home, or use the funds to pay for qualified higher education expenses.",
